**Conjecture 13.1 (Bertuzzo–Ravagnani–Yaakobi, Conjecture 3.2).** Let $K$ be any finite field of cardinality $q$, let $k\ge2$, and put $n=(q^k-1)/(q-1)$. For every rank-$k$ matrix $G\in K^{k\times n}$, and every matrix $S\in K^{k\times n}$ whose columns are exactly one nonzero representative of each projective line in $K^k$, let $X_0,X_1,\ldots$ be iid uniform physical positions in $\operatorname{Fin}(n)$. Write $T_C=\min\{t:\operatorname{span}_K(C_{X_0},\ldots,C_{X_{t-1}})=K^k\}$. Then

$$
\mathbb E[T_S]\le\mathbb E[T_G].
$$

**Scope and source.** This is arXiv:2603.06489v1, Section 3, Conjecture 3.2 (Problem B), with its predecessor in arXiv:2507.20639v1, Section III, unnumbered simplex-optimizer paragraph. The formal target is the original `MinimumRetrievalTime.retrievalTime`, under `MinimumRetrievalTime.uniformSamples (Fin n)`, and the real integral of its `toReal`. All zero, repeated and scalar-parallel competitor columns retain their original physical positions. No uniqueness, restricted-field, conditioned-sampling, rank-deficient or projective-only conclusion is asserted. Preregistration is issue #11799. The full formal target remains OPEN until its sole complete result is kernel-checked; the known simplex closed-form expectation is literature context, not a second settlement.

**Proof route.** Replace only zero columns of $G$ by one fixed nonzero vector on the same physical positions. The old prefix span is contained in the new prefix span: a zero vector belongs to every submodule, and every unchanged vector is a generator of the new span. The same argument preserves full ambient span. Sections 12.1–12.2 compare the resulting nonzero physical generator with uniform projective recovery. A bijection from the actual simplex positions to projective lines transports the spanning-filtered finite word sums, preserving singleton spans and the exact cardinality $n$. All-horizon recovery comparison gives the reverse comparison of measurable failure complements. The existing probability bridge identifies the actual nonnegative expectations with the sums of those tails and proves finiteness, integrability and real-integral conversion. Compare the ENNReal sums before taking `toReal`, using finiteness of the original generator's expectation. These steps are local to the one complete source-preregistered conclusion.
